mach_absolute_time starts at 0 at boot on macOS: Gunyah passes the host's counter through (no CNTVOFF), so the timebase
stood at the host's uptime. On a Mac it starts near 0, and macOS reads it so: HID idle time is now minus the last event
(none yet: 0), and a timebase hours past 0 put every display to sleep as soon as WindowServer came up. The boot CPU sets
rtclock_base_abstime the way wake does, the secondaries take it (cpu_timebase_init(FALSE)). Under XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX: the
iPadOS board has run on the host's counter from the start.
From macOS-DroidVM patches/xnu-12377/0018 (its cpu.c hunk; lateautumn233).

diff --git a/osfmk/arm64/cpu.c b/osfmk/arm64/cpu.c
index ee39d9a..236c656 100644
--- a/osfmk/arm64/cpu.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/cpu.c
@@ -1056,6 +1056,17 @@ cpu_timebase_init(boolean_t from_boot)
 		 */
 		rtclock_base_abstime = wake_abstime - ml_get_hwclock();
 	} else if (from_boot) {
+#if XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX
+		/*
+		 * The hypervisor passes the host's counter through (no CNTVOFF), so the timebase stood at the host's uptime
+		 * at boot. Start mach_absolute_time() at 0, as a Mac's does, the way wake does above: HID idle time is now minus
+		 * the last event (none yet: 0), and a timebase hours past 0 put every display to sleep as soon as WindowServer
+		 * came up. The secondary CPUs take this base (cpu_timebase_init(FALSE)).
+		 */
+		if (cdp == &BootCpuData) {
+			rtclock_base_abstime = 0 - ml_get_hwclock();
+		}
+#endif
 #if DEBUG || DEVELOPMENT
 		if (PE_parse_boot_argn("timebase_offset", &timebase_offset, sizeof(timebase_offset))) {
 			rtclock_base_abstime += timebase_offset;
